Training a model for biased, discriminatory practices, whether based on racial bias, sexual discrimination and the like goes contrary not only AI ethics and guidelines aimed primarily at fairness but fundamentally against the very tenets upon which society stands in promoting equality for everyone regardless of their identity, background, color or gender identity among others factors often associated historically or culturally based biases which should not influence decision-making mechanisms especially through technology or algorithms meant not just serve society but enhance it fairly across the board for everyone alike.

The creation, sharing, distribution or usage intention related to developing technology aimed at discrimination based on protected characteristics like one's skin color or sexual gender not aligns well neither with community policies here aimed primarily at providing helpful guidance based also on AI safety considerations nor societal norms worldwide, which increasingly recognize the dangers posed by bias in technology applications ranging widely into areas impacting daily life decisions, justice system processes, financial services among myriad of critical areas touching public life.

Instead focusing efforts on training AI models responsibly can contribute immensely in solving many pressing global challenges including but not confined solely to healthcare through personalized treatment predictions based on extensive health-related data; finance by offering equitable credit assessment mechanisms free from gender biases; legal systems aided by AI tools capable of fair and objective decision support minimizing biases inherent sometimes in judicial or investigative proceedings;
